\title[The Speculative-Decoding Cost Law]{The Speculative-Decoding Cost Law:\\
Survival Curves, Optimal Depth, and the Fragility of Differenced Estimators}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{25 August 2026}

\begin{abstract}
Speculative decoding accelerates autoregressive generation by having a cheap draft model
propose $d$ tokens which an expensive target model verifies in a single batched pass. The
\emph{speculation depth} $d$ is a free parameter, universally tuned by brute-force sweeping.
We give a complete structure theory for the throughput functional it controls.

Let $s_i$ be the probability that the accepted run survives past drafted position $i$, let
$A(d)=\sum_{i<d}s_i$ be the expected number of verified tokens per verification pass at depth
$d$, and let $c$ be the cost of drafting one token in units of one verification pass. The
\emph{cost law} is $G(d)=A(d)/(1+cd)$.

We prove: an exact one-step improvement criterion, $G(d)\le G(d+1)$ iff the marginal
$M(d)=s_d(1+cd)-cA(d)$ is nonnegative; an equivalent \emph{equilibrium law}
$M(d)<0\iff s_d<c\,G(d)$ equating a per-position microscopic quantity with an aggregate
macroscopic one; \emph{discrete concavity}
$M(d+1)-M(d)=(1+c(d+1))(s_{d+1}-s_d)$, whence unimodality of $G$ and exactness of myopic
stopping for nonincreasing survival; a strictly weaker \emph{single-crossing} criterion
certifying a global optimum with no monotonicity hypothesis; the universal ceiling
$G(d)<1/c$; existence of a finite optimum whenever cumulative acceptance is bounded and also
for the Zipf profile $s_i=1/(i+1)$ whose acceptance diverges; a $\Theta(\log(1/c))$ depth law
for geometric survival $s_i=r^i$ with matching bounds; the impossibility of a
workload-independent optimal depth; a sup-norm \emph{argmax stability} theorem
$|G_t(d)-G_s(d)|\le \varepsilon d/(1+cd)$; and a \emph{tight noise-amplification law} for the
differencing estimator $\hat s_i=(i+1)m(i+1)-i\,m(i)$, whose error can reach $(2i+1)\delta$
under aggregate error $\delta$, versus $d\delta$ for the cumulative statistic.

Instantiated on a fine depth sweep $d\in\{1,\dots,8\}$ over prose and code registers for a
$0.5$B draft model speculating for a CPU-hosted $7$B target at measured overhead $c=0.118$,
the cost-law argmax computed from extracted survival curves reproduces the independently
measured throughput optima exactly --- depth $4$ for prose, depth $8$ for code --- and the
single-crossing theorem upgrades both to global optima. Simultaneously the differenced curves
are not legal survival curves: three of sixteen values exceed $1$ and one is negative. The
amplification law identifies the estimator as the culprit, while argmax stability certifies a
robustness radius of $1/100$, wider than the winning margins $0.0764$ and $0.1468$. The
macroscopic conclusion is robust precisely where the microscopic one is not.
\end{abstract}

