\begin{result}[Register argmaxima]
Over $d\in\{1,\dots,8\}$: $G_\prose(d)<G_\prose(4)$ for every $d\ne4$, and
$G_\code(d)<G_\code(8)$ for every $d\ne8$. In particular $G_\prose(8)<G_\prose(4)$ while
$G_\code(4)<G_\code(8)$.
\end{result}

The cost-law argmax computed from timing-free acceptance data reproduces the independently
measured throughput optima exactly, in both registers, including the factor-of-two separation
--- Theorem~\ref{thm:impossible} realized in measurement.

\subsection{Upgrade to global optima}
The prose marginals are
\[
M(0)=0.670,\; M(1)=1.095,\; M(2)=0.316,\; M(3)=0.912,\; M(4)=-0.179,
\]
\[
M(5)=-0.289,\; M(6)=-0.425,\; M(7)=-0.188,
\]
and $M(e)=-c\,A(8)<0$ for $e\ge8$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:vanishing}: exactly one sign change, at
$d=4$.

\begin{result}[Global optimality]
$G_\prose(d)\le G_\prose(4)$ and $G_\code(d)\le G_\code(8)$ for every $d\in\mathbb{N}$.
\end{result}

Both follow from Theorem~\ref{thm:crossing} together with Theorem~\ref{thm:vanishing} for the
tail; for code the marginals are nonnegative throughout $\{0,\dots,7\}$ (e.g.\
$M(7)=0.690\cdot1.826-0.118\cdot6.260=0.521$). Theorem~\ref{thm:myopic} could \emph{not} have
been used: the measured curves are not nonincreasing. The single-crossing criterion is doing
real work.

\subsection{The loop closes: the equilibrium crossing}

\begin{result}[Equilibrium at the measured prose optimum]
$s_4=0.119<c\,G(4)=0.118\times2.038\approx0.241$, while $s_3=0.860\ge c\,G(3)\approx0.187$.
\end{result}

The optimum is literally the crossing of the per-position survival with $0.118\times$ the
achieved throughput, exactly as Theorem~\ref{thm:equilibrium} requires. Two experiments --- an
acceptance measurement with no clock, and a throughput measurement with no acceptance logging
--- are the same experiment.

\subsection{What went wrong: the extracted curves are not survival curves}

\begin{result}[Inadmissibility]
The prose curve is not nonincreasing, since $s_0=0.670<1.050=s_1$. Moreover
$s^\prose_1=1.050>1$, $s^\prose_6=-0.030<0$, $s^\code_1=1.120>1$ and $s^\code_3=1.060>1$. Of
the sixteen extracted values exactly three exceed $1$ and exactly one is negative; every other
value lies in $[0,1]$.
\end{result}

A survival probability cannot exceed $1$ or fall below $0$. The cause is
Theorem~\ref{thm:amplify}, not a coding error: with only four prompts per experimental cell,
the sup-norm error $\delta$ in the aggregate means is substantial, and differencing inflates
it to $(2i+1)\delta$ --- a factor of $13$ at position $6$ --- with
Theorem~\ref{thm:tight} showing no better bound is available in the worst case.

\subsection{Why the answer survived: certified robustness radius}

\begin{result}[Robustness]
Every sequence $t$ with $\|t-s^\prose\|_\infty\le1/100$ satisfies $G_{c,t}(d)<G_{c,t}(4)$ for
all $d\in\{1,\dots,8\}$, $d\ne4$; every $t$ with $\|t-s^\code\|_\infty\le1/100$ satisfies
$G_{c,t}(d)<G_{c,t}(8)$ for all $d\ne8$.
\end{result}

Both follow from Theorem~\ref{thm:argmax}, checking the margin condition at each competing
depth. The tightest margins are
$G_\prose(4)-G_\prose(5)=0.0764$ and $G_\code(8)-G_\code(7)=0.1468$, against perturbation
budgets $0.01\,(4/1.472+5/1.590)=0.0586$ and $0.01\,(8/1.944+7/1.826)=0.0794$.

The deliverable is asymmetric, and honestly so: \emph{the per-position estimates are wrong; the
argmax computed from them is right, with a certified robustness radius of $1/100$.} The
structural reason is that the cost law depends on $s$ only through partial sums, in which the
alternating differencing errors telescope.

\begin{result}[Sweep not saturated]
$G_\code(8)=3.575<1/c\approx8.475$ and $G_\code(7)<G_\code(8)$.
\end{result}

The code register is still improving at the sweep boundary and lies far below the universal
ceiling: the recorded optimum $8$ is an artefact of where the sweep stopped.

\section{Algorithms}

\subsection*{Myopic depth selection}
\begin{verbatim}
A <- 0
for d = 0, 1, ..., D-1:
    G <- A / (1 + c*d)              # current throughput
    if s[d] < c*G:  return d        # equilibrium crossing
    A <- A + s[d]
return D
\end{verbatim}
Complexity $O(D)$ arithmetic, $O(1)$ memory, zero timing runs. Correctness by
Theorems~\ref{thm:equilibrium} and \ref{thm:myopic} for nonincreasing $s$, and by
Theorem~\ref{thm:crossing} whenever the marginal changes sign once.

\subsection*{Certified argmax under measurement error}
\begin{verbatim}
compute G(d) for d = 0..D from the estimated curve
d0 <- argmax G
for each d != d0:
    budget <- eps*d/(1+c*d) + eps*d0/(1+c*d0)
    if budget >= G(d0) - G(d):  return (d0, UNCERTIFIED)
return (d0, CERTIFIED)
\end{verbatim}
A \texttt{CERTIFIED} verdict means, by Theorem~\ref{thm:argmax}, that every curve within
sup-distance $\varepsilon$ has argmax $d_0$. Both measured registers certify at
$\varepsilon=1/100$.

\subsection*{Geometric depth oracle}
For calibrated $r$ and $c$ the optimum is bracketed by
\[
d_{\mathrm{lo}}=\left\lfloor \frac{\log((1-r)/c)}{\log(1/r)}\right\rfloor,
\qquad
d_{\mathrm{hi}}=\min\{d\ge1: r^d(1+cd)<c\}.
\]

\section{Applications}

\emph{Replacing the sweep.} The dominant practical cost of tuning a speculative decoder is the
sweep; myopic depth selection replaces it with a single acceptance-logging run plus $O(D)$
arithmetic. The survival curve is hardware-independent while $c$ is not, so a curve measured
once is re-optimized for new hardware by changing one scalar, with monotone comparative
statics guaranteeing the optimum moves in a predictable direction.

\emph{Register-adaptive depth.} Theorem~\ref{thm:impossible} and the measured $4$-versus-$8$
split show a fixed depth leaves throughput on the table; the equilibrium test is cheap enough
to run online.

\emph{Draft-model selection.} The ceiling $G<1/c$ imposes hard diminishing returns on draft
accuracy, while the logarithmic depth law quantifies how much depth a lower $c$ buys: a
constant per halving.

\emph{Instrumentation design.} The amplification theorems are a design requirement for any
acceptance-logging harness: emit per-position accept/reject events, not just block means.

\emph{Beyond decoding.} Nothing in Sections 2--7 is specific to language models. The cost law
is the general form of ``batch a speculative pipeline whose success probability decays with
depth, against a fixed setup cost plus a linear per-item cost'': speculative execution windows,
prefetch depth, optimistic concurrency-control batch size, and pipelined query execution all
instantiate it.

\section{Discussion and Limitations}

Sections 2--7 are unconditional mathematics about the functional $G$; Section~\ref{sec:measured}
consists of claims about specific recorded vectors and the constant $c=0.118$. The global
optimality results assert optimality for the recorded curves \emph{extended by zero past
position $7$}, a modelling convention rather than a measurement; for code the sweep was still
improving at its boundary, so the data provides no evidence about depth $9$.

With four prompts per cell the differenced estimates are inadmissible; the argmax survives only
through argmax stability and the measured margins. A margin of $0.0764$ against a budget of
$0.0586$ is comfortable but not luxurious, and would not survive a doubling of $\varepsilon$.

The nonmonotone data is not merely a nuisance: it is why the single-crossing theorem exists.
Had only the monotone theory been developed, the entire optimality analysis would be
inapplicable to the actual measurement. Monotonicity is the natural assumption; single
crossing is the useful one.

The cost model $1+cd$ assumes drafting cost linear in depth and verification cost
depth-independent --- good approximations in the CPU-hosted regime measured here, degrading on
accelerators where attention cost grows. The first-order theory generalizes to any cost $C(d)$
via $s_d\,C(d)\ge(C(d+1)-C(d))A(d)$; the closed forms of Section 6 do not.

\section{Future Directions}

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{A sample-size law for a certified argmax.} The argmax is decided by the sup-norm
accuracy of the \emph{cumulative} means, and argmax stability converts that accuracy into an
explicit margin condition. A concentration bound on $m(d)$ over $n$ prompts would turn ``the
sweep says $4$'' into ``the population optimum is $4$ with confidence $1-\delta$'', with $n$ in
closed form from the measured margins ($0.0764$ prose, $0.1468$ code). This would retire the
sweep entirely.
\item \textbf{Direct per-position instrumentation.} Log accept/reject per position from the
verifier rather than differencing block means. With admissible curves in hand the full monotone
theory applies rather than only the single-crossing fragment, and decay hypotheses become
structural statements rather than inequalities on a recorded vector.
\item \textbf{Nonlinear and stochastic cost models,} covering accelerator deployments where the
flat-verification assumption fails.
\item \textbf{Register detection and online adaptation:} how cheaply can a register be
identified online, and how fast can the survival curve be re-estimated after a switch?
\item \textbf{Two-rate and mixture survival families.} A single geometric rate cannot reproduce
both measured optima (Proposition~\ref{prop:seven} gives $7$, between $4$ and $8$). A two-phase
family --- fast early decay, slow tail --- is the minimal model consistent with the split, and
its depth law is not yet known in closed form.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

The optimal speculation depth is not a hyperparameter to be swept but a fixed point to be
computed. The cost law $G(d)=A(d)/(1+cd)$ admits an exact one-step criterion, an equilibrium
reading in which a microscopic survival probability meets a macroscopic throughput, a discrete
concavity making greedy stopping globally exact, a $\Theta(\log(1/c))$ depth law, a universal
$1/c$ ceiling, and a proof that no depth serves all workloads.

Measured against a fine depth sweep on two registers, the theory reproduced both independently
measured throughput optima exactly --- $4$ for prose, $8$ for code --- from acceptance data
containing no timing information, and the single-crossing theorem upgraded both to global
optima. That the same measurement produced impossible per-position probabilities is not a
contradiction but a second result: the differencing estimator amplifies aggregate noise by a
tight factor of $2i+1$, while the argmax, depending only on partial sums, is stable within a
certified radius of $1/100$. Fragile estimates, robust conclusion --- and a theorem that says
exactly which is which.

